<article class="env-lemma" id="0AXI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AXI">Lemma <span data-tag="0AXI">15.127.6</span>.</a> Let $(A, \mathfrak m, \kappa )$ be a Noetherian normal local domain of dimension $2$. If $a \in \mathfrak m$ is nonzero, then there exists an element $c \in A$ such that $A/cA$ is reduced and such that $a$ divides $c^ n$ for some $n$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\text{div}(a) = \sum _{i = 1, \ldots , r} n_ i \mathfrak p_ i$ with notation as in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0AXH" data-tag="0AXH">15.127.5</a>. Choose $c \in \mathfrak p_1 \cap \ldots \cap \mathfrak p_ r$ with $A/cA$ reduced, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0AXH" data-tag="0AXH">15.127.5</a>. For $n \geq \max (n_ i)$ we see that $-\text{div}(a) + \text{div}(c^ n)$ is an effective divisor (all coefficients nonnegative). Thus $c^ n/a \in A$ by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/031T" data-tag="031T">10.157.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>